// Proposal 0179 Stage 5 contract: superclass evidence survives the module
// interface, so an inherited method dispatches the same way whether the
// defining module was just compiled or loaded from cache.
//
// `describe` is constrained on `Measurable` alone and calls `size`, which
// `Sizeable` declares. Reaching it means projecting the superclass evidence
// out of the `Measurable` dictionary — and the slot it sits in is decided by
// how many superclasses the *defining* module said `Measurable` has.
//
// A consumer that reloaded the interface without the superclass identities
// would lay the dictionary out with one slot instead of two and read `weight`
// where the evidence should be. That is why `superclass_class_modules` is
// carried alongside `superclasses` and must match it in length.
//
// Expected output:
// 505

import SuperclassAcrossModules.Scale exposing (size, weight)

fn describe<a: Measurable>(x: a) -> Int {
    size(x) + weight(x)
}

fn main() with IO {
    print(describe(5))
}
